There have been very few international assessments targeting the sustainable use of wild species and those past reports focused only on a few taxa and/or case studies . Nonetheless, previous biodiversity and sectoral assessments offer valuable insights into uses of wild species, their contributions to human wellbeing, and factors contributing to long-term prospects for their sustainable use. The sustainable use assessment builds on global assessments produced by IPBES, United Nations scientific organizations, and other global  organizations. It also includes one national assessment dedicated to non-timber forest products, since the practice of gathering (1.3.3) was not thoroughly reflected in the other global assessments. These assessments address human uses of wild species and factors contributing to their sustainability (or lack thereof) as a primary or secondary focus. 
      Collectively, the assessments document widespread global dependence on wild species for food, medicine, and other purposes . Contributions of use of wild species to good quality of life include direct provisioning, sources of income, and energy, the latter especially for cooking and sanitizing water . For many indigenous peoples and local communities, the use of wild species also are essential to identity and culture . These contributions of use of wild species to human wellbeing are continuous and quotidian, as well as episodic and exceptional. 
      The International Union of Forest Research Organizations estimates that nearly 20 % of the global population directly depend on forests, tree-based systems, and the wild species in them , while the FAO considers that fisheries and aquaculture support the livelihoods of 12 % of people around the world . In addition to serving as primary sources of subsistence resources, income, and identity, the use of wild species also serve as safety nets enabling survival during seasonal shortfalls and crises provoked by anthropogenic (e.g., armed conflicts) and non-anthropogenic (e.g., slow and rapid onset natural disasters) forces (see Chapter 4; . The use of wild species contributes also to the global economy. In the European Union alone, international legal trade of wild species is estimated at 100 billion euros worth importations .   
      These various contexts mean there are intra- and interannual variations in the frequency and intensity of the use of wild species throughout and across the years, and these variations may impact the sustainability of the use. Whether quotidian or exceptional, nearly all assessments note that human uses of wild species are particularly important to vulnerable individuals and groups including women, marginalized peoples, indigenous peoples, and forest-dependent communities (note that these may be overlapping rather than mutually exclusive categories;.
      Multiple assessments document the particularly important contributions of use of wild species to food security and nutrition . Consumption of wild animals, fungi, and plants contributes directly to human health and wellbeing through dietary quality and diversity, in rural and urban areas, in developed and developing countries . Reports to FAO from 91 countries with a fair geographical representation  showed in 2017 that over 2,800 distinct species are used for human food across the world, the largest share of wild species used for food coming from aquatic and forest production systems, with capture fisheries being likely the largest use of wild food by volume, for human consumption or feeding aquaculture species . A majority of wild fungi, insects, plants and, to a lesser extent, mammals and birds are obtained from forested ecosystems . Consumption of wild food also enables greater dietary choice (i.e., food sovereignty) and is often used as a complement to a limited set of cultivated crops . It has demonstrated benefits in terms of reducing metabolic diseases such as obesity and diabetes, which are epidemic in some parts of the world . In Madagascar, it was found that risk of aenemia in children from poor households was lowered by nearly 30% when the household consumed wild meat, even in small quantities (Golden 2011 in). Some assessments note that wild species are an important or primary source of protein  and allow people to get specific micronutrients. Examples show that, in many instances, wild species contain a much higher level of micronutrients, vitamins and proteins than similar domesticated crops or livestock . Beyond addressing primary needs, wild species may also be preferred to domestic species even when those latest are available through markets. Preference for wild species comes from traditional food preferences (e.g., for people moving from rural to urban areas) or “from a revival of interest in wild foods”, mainly because they are associated with cultural, recreational or nutritional nature’s contributions to people . Use of wild species may include risks for human health, through the transmission of diseases. Hunting, butchering, transportation and storage of live wild animals or their meat in unsafe conditions presents particularly risk of zoonotic diseases . 
      The reliance of biomedical processes and traditional medicine alike on wild species, and more specifically on wild plants is widely documented . Many biomedical innovations rely on wild species, but their development at an industrial production scale may threaten the preservation of the very resources underpinning traditional medicine. The Nagoya Protocol on Access to Genetic Resources and the Fair and Equitable Sharing of Benefits Arising from their Utilization aims, among other, to acknowledge communities’ traditional knowledge such as use of plants or animal parts for medicine, in order to protect their rights to those resources, which may include support for conservation and sustainable use on the ground or financial compensation for use of the resources by third parties. Wild species also contribute to mental health and well-being, including through extractive and non-extractive practices related to ceremonial and ritual, decorative and aesthetic, recreational and learning and educational uses . 
      Several assessments observe that in addition to land use/land cover change, unsustainable agriculture and forestry, pollution, and climate change, unsustainable uses of wild species are a primary driver of biodiversity loss . 
      Intensification of uses that had been sustainable in the past may lead them to become unsustainable if practices do not adapt to the new drivers (see Chapter 4, . Such drivers may influence:
* wild species populations (e.g., a disease affecting the wild species, or climate change leading to a shift in wild species range ; 
* the practices (e.g., technological change leading to more intensive or extensive harvesting, industrialization of a wild species use, commercial or demographic drivers leading to increased demand for a wild species, often entailing expansion from local to regional or global markets ; 
* the uses (e.g., new applications found for wild species, or shift from subsistence to commercial uses .
       For extractive practices, all those drivers may lead to an increased share of the biomass being removed from nature, thus disturbing the whole social-ecological system equilibrium. However, some assessments also note that much biodiversity is underpinned by the long-term results of indigenous and local knowledge and indigenous peoples’ and local communities’ customary practices associated with uses of wild species . Further, erosion of indigenous and local knowledge, in concert with indigenous peoples and local communities’ loss of access and tenure rights to traditional territories and resources appears to be a driver of unsustainable uses of wild species and biodiversity loss . All assessments in this review identify governance as an important factor in support of sustainable uses of wild species (see section 1.1.2 and 1.3 for a more detailed discussion on governance as a driver of the sustainable use of wild species). Several document positive ecological and social outcomes from decentralized, multi-sectoral approaches in which decision making and management authority do not rest solely at the level of the nation state . Rather, they recommend approaches that bring together the public and private sectors, non-governmental organizations, and local communities and integrate multi-sectoral actors from resource management, health, education, and others . Acknowledgement of and support for indigenous and local knowledge, tenure, and access to resources, including genetic resources, is identified as a key step toward sustainable use of wild species . Likewise, the importance of a rights-based approach with particular importance given to complying with international human rights laws and standards in all governance instruments and processes is noted as fundamental to governing the use of wild species .
      No assessment on non-extractive practices was identified, but some activities associated with nature-based tourism have been the focus of global reports .
      A study conducted in the early 2000s found that 20% to 40% of all international tourists included some form of wildlife watching in their trip. Tourism focusing on wild species sustains the whole tourism sector in some countries, and provides income and employment to people such as guides and drivers, as well as employees in the hotel and food services industries. Wildlife watching tourism “ranked as one of the top three export sectors for more than three-quarters of all developing countries in 2000, and was the principle export in a third of these countries” . Globally, “wildlife tourism” was estimated to represent 9% of gross domestic product in 2011 . Because protected areas are often wildlife watching destinations it also can support biodiversity conservation. It is therefore in the interest of national or local governments, but also of the tourism businesses, to support conservation of those areas. . 
      In the early 2000s,  noted that little was known about the actual income generated by wildlife watching or the impacts of tourism activities on the targeted species. Auditing of the activities around wildlife watching, to assess their ecological and social impacts, were still scarce . Another report by , focusing on coastal and marine tourism concluded, as did Tapper, that to avoid negative impacts, tourist density should be constrained. When nature-based tourism is over developed, it can have negative impacts on the wild species through increased disturbance, and on the local communities who risk being excluded from the benefits with the arrival of bigger, external players investing in tourism activities or through the commodification of people’s culture. The emphasis of the economy on wild species may also contribute to an increase in local conflicts between people and wild species . Further, if it becomes a main or single source of income, households and communities are vulnerable to the variations in tourist flows within and across the years. 
      The picture of the relationship between human uses of wild species and biodiversity that emerges from this review of previous assessments is far from simple. Opportunities for and outcomes of human uses of wild species are mediated by complex, interacting factors including environmental, economic, social, and cultural contexts . There are clearly documented instances in which the relationship between biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people through the use of wild species is reciprocal; nature’s contributions to people depend on biodiversity and much biodiversity is maintained through indigenous and local knowledge and practices related to the use of wild species. Equally, there are well-documented cases in which the use of wild species result in biodiversity losses. There are both rich sources of information on these processes and significant gaps in data and synthesis of existing knowledge from diverse sources. This impedes understanding of these processes and of the pathways to greater social and ecological sustainability of the use of wild species. 
Contributions of the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species  
The IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species is the first international endeavor to be fully comprehensive of all practices and uses associated with the sustainable uses of wild species. While many previous assessments focus on drivers of unsustainable uses of wild species and their consequences for biodiversity, the mandate of the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species is to identify solutions that enhance their sustainable uses. To do so, this assessment uses a groundbreaking conceptualization that enables analysis of any type of use of any species (see the organizing structure in section 1.3.4) and supports analyses across scales and comparison of successes and failures of interventions across practices. The framing of the analysis within the sustainability issue is also a novel aspect of this assessment. Building on assessments that have come before, the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species approaches the task through systematic review of the scientific literature and and addressing and working with indigenous and local knowledge to fill gaps in knowledge and identify pathways to enhanced sustainability of the use of wild species that contribute to conservation and development. 
      Regarding fishing and logging, the sustainable use assessment draws from the work of the FAO and especially the State of the World's Forests and the State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture, which focus on the resources (respectively, timber and non-timber forest products, and fish and shellfish,), in order to present the various uses associated with these species and where, how and why those uses are sustainable. Regarding terrestrial animal harvesting, gathering and non-extractive uses, the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species similarly draws the bigger picture related to those practices, while also collating for the first time global data and evidence on those species' uses. This assessment also gives a clear picture of where gaps are in data and synthesis of existing knowledge around the sustainable use of wild species (see supplementary material S1.1). As those gaps are filled, understanding of pathways to greater social-ecological sustainability of the use of wild species will improve.

""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "1.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "The IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species in the context of other assessments" .

